*(Make sure to add filterable: false to your creatorId, requestDate, requestCount, and actions columns as well to keep the filter dropdown clean).*
### 3. Add the Toolbar to the DataGrid
Finally, inject the inbuilt toolbar into your DataGrid component so the Filter button becomes visible.
**Find your <DataGrid> component near the bottom and update the slots prop:**
```javascript
        <DataGrid
          rows={rows}
          columns={columns}
          loading={loading}
          getRowId={(row) => row.batchId}
          disableRowSelectionOnClick
          pageSizeOptions={[5, 10, 25, 50]}
          disableColumnResize
          rowHeight={52}
          paginationMode="server"
          rowCount={totalRowCount}
          paginationModel={paginationModel}
          onPaginationModelChange={setPaginationModel}
          
          // --- UPDATE THE SLOTS PROP HERE ---
          slots={{ 
            noRowsOverlay: CustomNoRowsOverlay,
            toolbar: GridToolbar // <--- Adds the inbuilt filter/export toolbar
          }}
          // (Optional) Clean up the toolbar to only show the filter button
          slotProps={{
            toolbar: {
              printOptions: { disableToolbarButton: true },
              csvOptions: { disableToolbarButton: true },
              showQuickFilter: false,
            }
          }}
          
          sx={styles.dataGridContainer}
        />

```
### ⚠️ Important Note on Server-Side Pagination
Because your grid is currently using paginationMode="server" (meaning it only asks the Java backend for exactly 10 rows at a time), **this inbuilt frontend filter will only search through the 10 rows currently visible on your screen.**
If you want the filter to search your entire Oracle database, we will need to change filterMode="server" and pass the filter parameters (Batch ID/VID) directly into your Java getAllRequestsSummary SQL query.
